App Store submission: be the reviewer before Apple is. Repo /Users/x/Documents/edraft, branch rename/edraft, HEAD 28aaf8d (verify git status; never stage xcscheme, mcp.log, the root PNGs). CLAUDE.md discipline: lock_draft → Vibe Check → wait for my yes → run_locked → report. No commits, no co-author trailers. This is a READ-ONLY audit: your budget is exactly one new file, docs/APP-STORE-READINESS.md. Every other path is denied. No fixes in this lock — findings only.
Review this app as an Apple App Reviewer would, for macOS first (iOS follows later). Work through the App Review Guidelines sections that apply to a sandboxed document app with iCloud sync and no accounts: 2.1 app completeness, 2.3 accurate metadata, 2.4 hardware compatibility, 2.5 software requirements (sandbox, entitlements, no private APIs), 3.1 business (no IAP — confirm nothing trips it), 5.1 privacy (privacy manifest, privacy policy, data-collection declarations vs what the code actually does).
For each finding: severity — BLOCKS SUBMISSION / SHOULD FIX / POLISH — the evidence (file:line or the measured behavior), and the concrete remedy. Verify these known suspects and find the ones we don't know:
docs/privacy-policy.html still carries a literal [SUPPORT EMAIL] placeholder; the support/privacy URLs for App Store Connect don't exist yet
MARKETING_VERSION / CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION in apple/eDraft.xcodeproj — current values, and what 1.0 requires
ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption absent from apple/macOS/Info.plist (standard-library crypto only — say what App Store Connect needs answered)
apple/macOS/MainMenu.swift:34 registers "eDraft Help" → showHelp with no help book — a menu item that does nothing is a 2.1 completeness rejection risk
Entitlements: read apple/macOS/*.entitlements — exactly what we ask for vs what we use (iCloud container, sandbox, file access classes); flag anything requested-but-unused or used-but-unrequested
Document types: UTIs for .fountain/.fdx/.draft declared and consistent across Info.plist and the project
Signing/notarization readiness for a direct archive: DEVELOPMENT_TEAM Z77253BSS4, what a Release archive will need that Debug doesn't
What a reviewer sees in the first five minutes: launch → new document → type → save → open sample .fdx — note anything that reads as placeholder, broken, or unfinished (you may run swift tests and builds to verify, but do not modify)
Report format: a verdict line (would this pass review today? no/yes-with-notes), then findings ordered by severity, then a submission checklist in execution order. Proof: the file exists and covers every section above; zero other diffs. The value of this lock is that a second pass by a different agent finds nothing new — write it that well.